{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"fdebe8cefd00004d","name":"card_event_claim_the_skies","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/WinterWar/events/card_event_claim_the_skies","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"CLAIM THE SKIES","text":"Your air unit with the highest attack gets +1+1, then fights target enemy unit.","flavorText":"At the beginning of WWII, the Soviet Air Forces faced shortcomings with outdated aircraft and less advanced technology compared to Western nations. Despite having the world's largest air force in 1938, their aeronautical capabilities were notably inferior and were no match to the German Luftwaffe. However, by 1943, the Soviet Union had transformed their air force and managed to secure air superiority over Germany on the Eastern Front. By then their air forces were equipped with excellent fighters and ground attack aircraft, and outpaced the Germans in both quality and quantity. At the same time, the Luftwaffe withdrew numerous fighter squadrons from the Eastern Front in order to defend against Western Allied bombing raids, leaving the Eastern skies vulnerable to relentless air attacks. In 1944 and 1945, the Luftwaffe faced further challenges from dwindling resources, especially after the loss of the crucial Romanian oil fields that were now in the hands of the Soviet Union. As a result, by the end of the war, many German aircraft were just sitting on airfields without enough fuel to fly them.","faction":"Soviet","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_claim_the_skies","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_claim_the_skies_title","SourceString":"CLAIM THE SKIES","LocalizedString":"CLAIM THE SKIES"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_claim_the_skies_text","SourceString":"Your air unit with the highest attack gets +1+1, then fights target enemy unit.","LocalizedString":"Your air unit with the highest attack gets +1+1, then fights target enemy unit."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_claim_the_skies_history","SourceString":"At the beginning of WWII, the Soviet Air Forces faced shortcomings with outdated aircraft and less advanced technology compared to Western nations. Despite having the world's largest air force in 1938, their aeronautical capabilities were notably inferior and were no match to the German Luftwaffe. However, by 1943, the Soviet Union had transformed their air force and managed to secure air superiority over Germany on the Eastern Front. By then their air forces were equipped with excellent fighters and ground attack aircraft, and outpaced the Germans in both quality and quantity. At the same time, the Luftwaffe withdrew numerous fighter squadrons from the Eastern Front in order to defend against Western Allied bombing raids, leaving the Eastern skies vulnerable to relentless air attacks. In 1944 and 1945, the Luftwaffe faced further challenges from dwindling resources, especially after the loss of the crucial Romanian oil fields that were now in the hands of the Soviet Union. As a result, by the end of the war, many German aircraft were just sitting on airfields without enough fuel to fly them.","LocalizedString":"At the beginning of WWII, the Soviet Air Forces faced shortcomings with outdated aircraft and less advanced technology compared to Western nations. Despite having the world's largest air force in 1938, their aeronautical capabilities were notably inferior and were no match to the German Luftwaffe. However, by 1943, the Soviet Union had transformed their air force and managed to secure air superiority over Germany on the Eastern Front. By then their air forces were equipped with excellent fighters and ground attack aircraft, and outpaced the Germans in both quality and quantity. At the same time, the Luftwaffe withdrew numerous fighter squadrons from the Eastern Front in order to defend against Western Allied bombing raids, leaving the Eastern skies vulnerable to relentless air attacks. In 1944 and 1945, the Luftwaffe faced further challenges from dwindling resources, especially after the loss of the crucial Romanian oil fields that were now in the hands of the Soviet Union. As a result, by the end of the war, many German aircraft were just sitting on airfields without enough fuel to fly them."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::WinterWar","isReserved":true,"extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Fight"],"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Soviet/CandidateArtwork/t_red_air_order.t_red_air_order","SubPathString":""},"Delay":6.0,"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/CLAIM_THE_SKIES.CLAIM_THE_SKIES","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":277.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_claim_the_skies_photo","SourceString":"Soviet Yak-9 fighters during the Battle of Kursk, 1943.","LocalizedString":"Soviet Yak-9 fighters during the Battle of Kursk, 1943."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_OrderDiveBomber.BP_OrderDiveBomber_C","SubPathString":""},"isDirectDamage":true},"variables":[{"name":"friendlyAttack","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"myUnit","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"cardToCheck","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_claim_the_skies_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam1","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam2","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"targetedCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"out"}],"pure":false,"event":true,"flags":"FUNC_Event | FUNC_Public | FUNC_Has